  return $.response[200].json($.body).xml($.body);
};
```

Maybe I'm being too clever here, making sure you specify a response for both "application/json" and "application/xml" content types.

@programm-ingovals in your case, I see your OpenAPI has both "application/json" and "text/json". **This is a bug in Counterfact, and I'll fix it today**, but let me explain what's going on. 

If you write this, it should type check:

```ts
export const GET: HTTP_GET = ($) => {
  return $.response[200]
     .match("application/json", $.context.lansumsoknir)
     .match("text/json", $.context.lansumsoknir)
}
```

`.json(x)` is a shortcut for `.match("application/json", x)`. The bug is that it _only_ covers "application/json". It should also cover "text/json" and "text/x-json", if we want to cover all the [official and unofficial MIME types](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22406077/what-is-the-exact-difference-between-content-type-text-json-and-application-jso).
